# Post-mortem forensics for the shipping kernel: keep the kernel's last words in DRAM so an
# unexplained reset can be explained after the fact.
#
# The board's only recovery from a CPU-side hang is the hardware watchdog, and the reset it
# performs destroys every volatile record of what went wrong. ml-logd writes to a tmpfs, the
# console goes to a serial port nobody is attached to in flight, and the printk ring is RAM the
# next kernel overwrites. So a unit that resets in the field arrives back with no evidence at all.
#
# ramoops@29100000 (board DTS) is the way out: DRAM keeps its contents across a watchdog reset,
# measured bit-exact on this hardware, so what the kernel wrote before it died is still there when
# SPL boots the next one, which republishes it as /sys/fs/pstore. glue/dev/pstore-harvest.sh
# reads it.
#
# Costs the 1 MiB the DTS reserves and, with ramoops left as a module, nothing measurable in the
# kernel slot. The debug-freeze fragment builds on this for a bench hunt; this is the part that
# ships.

# The pstore core is built in; the ramoops backend stays a MODULE on purpose. Building it in
# costs about 57 KB of a slot already at 96%, almost all of it the Reed-Solomon tables
# PSTORE_RAM hard-selects and that this board never uses (the DTS sets no ecc-size). Loading
# ramoops from the rootfs a few seconds into boot costs nothing and loses only the window
# between kernel start and module load. Recovery of the PREVIOUS boot's records is unaffected:
# the data sits in DRAM and is read when the module probes, however late that is.
CONFIG_PSTORE=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM=m
# The primary record. Every printk is copied into the persistent ring as it is emitted, so the
# lines leading up to a hang are already in DRAM when the reset takes the machine.
CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE=y
# /dev/pmsg0, so userspace can leave breadcrumbs in the same region. ml-pipeline writes a
# per-frame phase trail here when /usrdata/missinglynk/pmsg exists; the zone costs nothing when
# nothing writes to it.
CONFIG_PSTORE_PMSG=y
# Records are kept uncompressed: a compressed record is unreadable if the dump was cut short
# mid-write, which is exactly the case this fragment exists to capture.
# CONFIG_PSTORE_COMPRESS is not set
